Open URLs through Lipstick, as Qt apps do.

Sailjail's sandbox leaves apps no way of their own to open a URL: there's no xdg-open in it, and no permission allows Lipstick's org.sailfishos.fileservice D-Bus service. Qt apps send the URL to the compositor with the qt_windowmanager Wayland extension instead, and Lipstick opens it with libcontentaction, outside the sandbox.

The runtime now binds qt_windowmanager (protocol file from QtWayland 5.15, BSD like surface-extension.xml) and offers it on the JSON method channel flutter-sfos/open_url: openUrl with the URL returns false if the compositor doesn't offer it. Long URLs go in 128-byte parts, as Qt sends them, each ending on a whole UTF-8 character. Checking that an app handles the URL is left to the caller.

